% XXX teaching set theory.
% Realized several things that feel like there really "wants" to be a universe of discourse, i.e. global set which other sets under consideration are subsets of.
%   - Complement of course doesn't make sense without this
%   - I introduced set equality by defining A = B by (A \subseteq B) \land (B \subseteq A).  But I realized this morning that
%     sometimes we would really like to treat set equality extensionally: i.e. (A = B) \iff (\forall x. x \in A \iff x \in B).  But what are we quantifying over?  Universal set.
%   - Subset itself: defined (A \subseteq B) as (\forall x \in A. x \in B) but maybe nicer to define it as (\forall x \in U. (x \in A) \to (x \in B)).  If we define it that way,
%     the extensional and subset-both-ways definitions of set equality are simply related by the fact that forall distributes over and:
%     (\forall x: U. (x \in A) \iff (x \in B)) == (\forall x: U. ((x \in A) \implies (x \in B)) \land ((x \in B) \implies (x \in A))) \equiv ((\forall x: U. (x \in A) \implies (x \in B)) \land (\forall x: U. (x \in B) \implies (x \in A))) \equiv (A \subseteq B) \land (B \subseteq A)
%
% But saying "there always has to be a universal set U" is kind of equivalent to saying that we want our sets to be _typed_: you can't just have "a set", you have to
% say what universe the elements are going to be chosen from, i.e. the type of the elements.
%
% What I would really like to do is (1) start with type theory, then later (2) introduce sets as #{Set(T) = T \to 2}, intrinsically typed, and build up set theory on that.
% Maybe I will do that someday...

